1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,966
To the untrained eye, this connect 4 position might look unassuming.

2
00:00:06,033 --> 00:00:10,879
This one, you might not even be able to tell who is winning.

3
00:00:11,966 --> 00:00:16,812
But an expert could tell you, all of these positions share a special property,

4
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:20,994
one which is actually quite extraordinary.

5
00:00:21,066 --> 00:00:26,776
One feature of all of these positions is that the amount of space remaining in each column is even.

6
00:00:26,833 --> 00:00:29,807
This column has 6 empty spots.

7
00:00:29,866 --> 00:00:32,384
This column has 4.

8
00:00:32,466 --> 00:00:36,064
This is true for all of the games I've shown.

9
00:00:36,133 --> 00:00:40,403
Not a single column with an odd number of pieces.

10
00:00:40,466 --> 00:00:46,272
What's more, in each of these cases, it so happens that Yellow, player 2, is winning.

11
00:00:46,333 --> 00:00:47,691
It might not look like it,

12
00:00:47,691 --> 00:00:50,620
 but Yellow is winning so spectacularly in these games,

13
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:52,683
 that even a fool could beat an expert.

14
00:00:52,683 --> 00:00:56,045
 As long as that fool knows what makes these positions special.

15
00:00:56,266 --> 00:00:59,312
Remember how all of the empty space is even?

16
00:00:59,366 --> 00:01:04,356
That means that we can cut those remaining columns up into groups of 2.

17
00:01:04,433 --> 00:01:10,887
Now, since it's red's turn in all of these cases, let's let Red start by making a move.

18
00:01:10,966 --> 00:01:16,436
When drawn like this, yellow is almost beckoned to fill in the uncoupled pair.

19
00:01:16,499 --> 00:01:22,401
And, since the entire board is divied up into pairs, Yellow can just keep doing this forever.

20
00:01:22,466 --> 00:01:27,816
Wherever Red goes, yellow fills in the unpaired spot.

21
00:01:27,899 --> 00:01:30,513
We can even take away the pairings.

22
00:01:30,566 --> 00:01:35,724
All Yellow is doing is responding, thoughtlessly, to Red's last move.

23
00:01:35,799 --> 00:01:42,277
A randomly placed red stone, is followed by a Yellow stone immediately above.

24
00:01:42,333 --> 00:01:50,059
So on, and so forth. [silence]

25
00:01:50,133 --> 00:01:53,323
Wait a minute... did yellow just win?

26
00:01:53,399 --> 00:01:58,965
Let's try again, with a different board which we saw at the beginning.

27
00:01:59,033 --> 00:02:05,151
Remember, nothing fancy. Red initiates, then Yellow naively responds.

28
00:02:05,233 --> 00:02:12,431
On and on, until the end of the game. [silence]

29
00:02:12,499 --> 00:02:15,569
Like magic, Yellow has won again.

30
00:02:15,633 --> 00:02:20,263
Now, I promised you those boards at the beginning had something magical about them.

31
00:02:20,333 --> 00:02:26,355
Does the columns having an even amount of empty space necessitate this winning condition for Yellow?

32
00:02:26,433 --> 00:02:30,151
Yellow is winning in this position.

33
00:02:30,233 --> 00:02:33,879
and it also has all even columns.

34
00:02:33,933 --> 00:02:40,963
However, it is not one of the special ones I showed before. Let's try our strategy here!

35
00:02:41,033 --> 00:02:44,967
Just like before, red then yellow.

36
00:02:45,033 --> 00:02:50,023
But with this last Red move, Yellow is starting to panic.

37
00:02:50,099 --> 00:02:55,233
If Yellow continues our simple strategy, Red will immediately win.

38
00:03:01,366 --> 00:03:05,972
So, Yellow, in a last ditch effort, breaks our strategy.

39
00:03:06,033 --> 00:03:10,807
Perhaps, by making some threats on the right side, Yellow can regain the tempo?

40
00:03:14,899 --> 00:03:20,033
Unfortunately for Yellow, the damage is already done.

41
00:03:20,099 --> 00:03:23,241
Red immediately plays in the second column...

42
00:03:23,299 --> 00:03:27,809
Yellow is forced to block the horizontal threat,

43
00:03:27,866 --> 00:03:32,736
but Red wins on a diagonal anyways.

44
00:03:34,833 --> 00:03:40,903
So, our naive just-follow-red strategy didn't work in this case,

45
00:03:40,966 --> 00:03:46,676
Despite the fact that yellow is winning, and all of the columns are even.

46
00:03:46,733 --> 00:03:54,099
Indeed, it is the case that the 4 boards at the beginning weren't just any boards with all even columns.

47
00:03:54,166 --> 00:03:56,177
They were especially selected,

48
00:03:56,177 --> 00:04:00,522
 as boards which can be won with our simple follow-red strategy.

49
00:04:00,633 --> 00:04:04,228
If Yellow could always win by just playing call-and-response,

50
00:04:04,228 --> 00:04:06,389
 that wouldn't be much fun, would it?

51
00:04:06,499 --> 00:04:10,985
So, what is it about these positions which makes them special?

52
00:04:11,066 --> 00:04:15,648
When we play our naive strategy, let's take note of what happens.

53
00:04:15,699 --> 00:04:19,849
Let's mark all of the disks already played before we start.

54
00:04:26,966 --> 00:04:31,980
Regardless of what Red chooses, after the point where we start to use our strategy,

55
00:04:32,033 --> 00:04:36,447
all of the red disks end up on an odd row.

56
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:41,394
That is, the first, third, and fifth rows from the bottom.

57
00:04:41,466 --> 00:04:45,328
Similarly, yellow inevitably gets all of the even rows.

58
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:51,014
Since Yellow is the one employing this strategy, let's call it 'claimeven'.

59
00:04:51,066 --> 00:04:57,544
Yellow, by merely following Red, can get hold of all of the remaining even-row spaces.

60
00:04:57,600 --> 00:04:59,550
What makes this board special,

61
00:04:59,550 --> 00:05:04,033
 is that even when we fill in all of the Red pieces on the odd rows,

62
00:05:04,033 --> 00:05:05,850
 no line of 4 disks is made.

63
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,558
However, Yellow is able to lay claim to the entire top row.

64
00:05:10,633 --> 00:05:13,517
So, it doesn't matter the order which Red plays.

65
00:05:13,517 --> 00:05:18,357
 The result is already guaranteed for Yellow, as long as Yellow plays Claimeven.

66
00:05:18,466 --> 00:05:21,488
Well, what about this slightly different board?

67
00:05:21,566 --> 00:05:27,468
It was another one of the 4 at the beginning, so it also wins by claimeven.

68
00:05:27,533 --> 00:05:31,203
If we imagine all the red stones filling in the odd rows...

69
00:05:31,266 --> 00:05:33,856
There's a red line of 4!

70
00:05:33,933 --> 00:05:39,499
But... in this case, it is hovering immediately over a Yellow line of 4.

71
00:05:39,566 --> 00:05:43,620
So, for Red to win... Yellow would already have to have won.

72
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:47,418
We say that the red line of 4 is undercut.

73
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:49,538
Let's play it out!

74
00:05:56,633 --> 00:06:01,119
Sure enough, we win on the precise line that we predicted!

75
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:03,513
This is what makes these boards special.

76
00:06:03,513 --> 00:06:06,063
 If there are red winning lines on odd rows,

77
00:06:06,063 --> 00:06:09,618
 they are all undercut by yellow winning chains on even rows.

78
00:06:10,833 --> 00:06:13,063
Let's try some examples.

79
00:06:13,133 --> 00:06:17,907
It's Yellow's turn. How can we force a win using Claimeven?

80
00:06:17,966 --> 00:06:20,628
Pause now if you want to think about it.

81
00:06:22,733 --> 00:06:27,987
By placing this disk in the center column, Yellow can kill two birds with one stone.

82
00:06:28,066 --> 00:06:30,944
This move makes all of the columns even,

83
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:37,070
and guarantees that Red can't make a line of 4 until after Yellow gets a line of 4 on the 4th row.

84
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:46,246
Let's try this trickier example.

85
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:50,906
We may naively play the spot which yields even amounts of empty space in each column.

86
00:06:50,966 --> 00:06:57,060
But red has other plans in mind. Let's see what happens if we try to play Claimeven.

87
00:06:57,133 --> 00:07:00,371
Red claims this spot on the seventh column.

88
00:07:00,433 --> 00:07:05,759
See how that makes this threat on an odd row? Pay attention to that threat.

89
00:07:05,833 --> 00:07:11,327
Oblivious, Yellow continues playing Claimeven. Let's see what happens.

90
00:07:13,433 --> 00:07:16,095
Well, that didn't work.

91
00:07:16,166 --> 00:07:19,620
So then, what was yellow supposed to do?

92
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:22,194
Pause now if you want to think about it.

93
00:07:24,300 --> 00:07:29,074
The trick is blocking that third row threat before it comes to fruition.

94
00:07:29,133 --> 00:07:33,331
Now, most columns have an even amount of empty space.

95
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:35,654
But two columns don't.

96
00:07:35,733 --> 00:07:38,515
Is there any hope of using Claimeven?

97
00:07:38,566 --> 00:07:42,260
It turns out, here, the trick is as follows:

98
00:07:42,333 --> 00:07:47,251
If Red plays in one of these spots, then Yellow plays the other.

99
00:07:47,333 --> 00:07:51,075
From there, we can continue with Claimeven.

100
00:07:56,166 --> 00:08:00,724
Reducing a complex position to a simple one is difficult.

101
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:05,070
However, an expert player up against a newbie might make it look easy.

102
00:08:05,133 --> 00:08:10,507
As expected, capitalizing on these situations on the fly is the hallmark of expertise.

103
00:08:10,566 --> 00:08:13,468
Attentive practice can help you see it coming.

104
00:08:15,566 --> 00:08:16,422
Before we part,

105
00:08:16,422 --> 00:08:20,962
 I'll give you a taste of how this can be generalized, with one last puzzle.

106
00:08:21,066 --> 00:08:25,776
In this case, and unlike the others, Red, AKA player 1, is winning.

107
00:08:25,776 --> 00:08:28,070
 But for now, it's Yellow's turn.

108
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:32,806
How can we apply our understanding of claimeven to this position?

109
00:08:32,866 --> 00:08:39,128
For one, we notice that there is precisely one column that doesn't have an even amount of spaces left.

110
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:42,438
This means we can't play claimeven.

111
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:44,154
If Yellow goes anywhere but the middle,

112
00:08:44,233 --> 00:08:47,927
we find that there are two even spaces we need to take.

113
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,246
So, Claimeven doesn't exactly work when you are player 1.

114
00:08:52,300 --> 00:08:56,282
Well, what happens if we just naively respond to Yellow anyways?

115
00:08:56,333 --> 00:09:01,731
We'll follow this pattern here, Red will get the top of each coupled pair of spaces.

116
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:02,873
In other words,

117
00:09:02,873 --> 00:09:09,116
 Red will play Claimeven, except, on the center column, it will instead be Claimodd.

118
00:09:09,233 --> 00:09:14,391
But, something weird is going on on the middle column, there's an unpaired spot...

119
00:09:14,466 --> 00:09:18,760
One other thing we notice is that there is an odd-row red threat on the center column.

120
00:09:18,833 --> 00:09:24,615
Since that is on the top half of one of the coupled spaces, Red will get that space.

121
00:09:24,666 --> 00:09:26,539
That means the game will end there,

122
00:09:26,539 --> 00:09:27,478
 and consequently,

123
00:09:27,478 --> 00:09:31,548
 we don't need to worry about what happens on the top of the middle column.

124
00:09:31,700 --> 00:09:37,098
Alright, let's try it! Claimeven, except claimodd in the middle.

125
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:46,910
Once again, this only works because this position was, in some sense, simple to begin with.

126
00:09:46,966 --> 00:09:53,684
Red, through training and expertise, was able to force a position which permits such a simple strategy.

127
00:09:53,766 --> 00:09:55,802
Most positions aren't like this-

128
00:09:55,802 --> 00:10:00,770
 most positions can't be naively solved just using Claimeven and/or Claimodd.

129
00:10:00,900 --> 00:10:02,702
But let this serve as a taste,

130
00:10:02,702 --> 00:10:10,928
 an idea from which we could devise a more expressive positional language which permits the solution of a much larger class of boards.

131
00:10:11,033 --> 00:10:15,267
Understanding these positions is not only crucial for the learner,

132
00:10:15,267 --> 00:10:17,749
 but also for the connect 4 researcher.

133
00:10:17,866 --> 00:10:22,712
Solving a game requires an immense search of billions of options on behalf of either player.

134
00:10:22,766 --> 00:10:33,156
However, these positions from which a player can win via Claimeven prove to be a small island of simplicity in an otherwise chaotic sea of complex situations.

135
00:10:33,233 --> 00:10:39,567
These positions will serve as a foothold, an indispensable landmark for further reduction.

136
00:10:39,633 --> 00:10:42,871
Stay tuned! This has been 2swap.

